{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause identified — hypothesis 1 confirmed, with the exact writer named\n\nThe `.git/config` rewrite at 17:36 was performed by a **concurrent Claude Code session (PID 12747, started 17:01:41 PDT)**, via a shell it spawned at **17:36:09 PDT** — matching the recorded `.git/config` mtime to the minute.\n\n### Evidence\n\n`ps -Ao pid,ppid,lstart,command` run 2026-10-27 21:34 PDT shows PID 30648 (parent 12747), started `Tue Oct 27 17:36:09 2026`, running:\n\n```sh\nsc=$(ls -d $PWD/.rebar/scratch/df72-*) && \\\nfor r in $(git -C $sc/tracker-copy remote); do git -C $sc/tracker-copy remote remove $r; done\n```\n\n`$PWD` was the worktree `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/worktrees/fix-df72-stale-snapshot-close`, so `$sc` = `.../.rebar/scratch/df72-23558`.\n\nThe intent was clearly to strip remotes from a *throwaway copy* of the tracker. It did not do that. `tracker-copy` was produced by a **file copy of the real tracker worktree**, and that copy carried the real worktree's `.git` **file** verbatim:\n\n```\n$ cat .../df72-23558/tracker-copy/.git\ngitdir: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker\n\n$ git -C .../df72-23558/tracker-copy rev-parse --git-dir --git-common-dir\n/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker\n/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git\n```\n\nThat path is **absolute**. A copied worktree is not a copy of a repository — it is a second pointer at the original. So `git -C tracker-copy remote` listed the SHARED repository's remotes (`origin`, `gerrit`), and `git remote remove` wrote to the SHARED `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/config`. That is exactly the observed damage: both `[remote \"...\"]` sections gone, `[core]`, `[gc]`, `[maintenance]`, `[merge \"ours\"]`, `[user]`, `[credential]` all intact, because `git remote remove` edits only the sections it owns.\n\n### Status of the three recorded hypotheses\n\n1. **Concurrent session / operator action** — CONFIRMED, with the process, its parent, its start time, and its command line.\n2. **A tool that rewrites `.git/config` wholesale** — REFUTED. Nothing rewrote the file wholesale; `git remote remove` surgically deleted two sections, which is why everything else survived. The surgical-survival pattern that looked like \"a tool that did not model those sections\" is just git's own section editing.\n3. **Related to the 18:40 `git timed out after 30s` lock fault** — correlation only, and now explainable: the same runaway process (see below) holds a rebar write lock and runs git operations against the shared object store, so git contention on this host at that time is expected rather than coincidental.\n\n### This is not isolated — the same process also caused bug `painted-epidermal-shelduck` (`f1a2-0755-693d-4924`)\n\nThe same shell then ran `converge_snapshot_close_metadata_unit(tracker-copy)` under `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off REBAR_SYNC_PULL=off`. Because `tracker-copy` resolves to the real tracker's git-dir with `HEAD -> refs/heads/tickets`, its REBUILD SNAPSHOT commits landed on the **real local `tickets` branch** and were never pushed. The `tickets` reflog shows exactly 4 such commits (18:56:20, 19:43:57, 20:03:05, 20:53:16) with no intervening `reset: moving to origin/tickets` — which is the 4-ahead/32-behind divergence that ticket reports.\n\n**The process is still running as of 21:35 PDT (elapsed 3h58m)**, with child PID 30670 still executing `run_unit.py`. It belongs to another session; this session has not signalled it. It may make further commits to the shared `tickets` branch.\n\n### What this means for the fix\n\nThe AC \"identify the writer\" is met. The remaining ACs are prevention, and the generalisable defect is now sharper than the original framing: **copying a linked git worktree directory produces a second live handle on the original repository, not an isolated copy.** Any \"work on a throwaway copy of the tracker\" recipe built with `cp -r` is unsafe by construction — it can rewrite the shared config, move shared refs, and take shared locks. A `make worktree` preflight that names a missing `origin` is still worth having, but it treats the symptom; the durable guard is making the shared checkout's remote configuration recoverable and warning about copied-worktree handles.\n\nNext: proposing the concrete remediation on this ticket before claiming."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793162180624783043,"uuid":"2437c61b-cbb2-4e18-9f0a-37eea85dbb72"}